WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:34.000
This is Hidden Brain. I'm Shankar Vedanta. In the middle of a workday in April 2013, the Associated Press sent out a tweet breaking two explosions in the White House, it read. Barack Obama is injured. The tweet caused panic in the financial markets. Within minutes, the Dow plunged more than a hundred points as traders rushed to sell off stocks amid the threat of political instability. The news was bogus. There were no explosions, and the president was safe.

2
00:00:34.000 --> 00:01:10.000
The AP's Twitter account had been hacked. Within a few minutes, the information was corrected, and the markets recovered. When we think of misinformation, we often think of stories like this. Blatant cases of lies and falsehood, perpetrated by deceitful and malevolent agents. But in recent years, social scientists have found that misinformation comes in many flavors, most of which are far subtler than the erroneous AP story.

3
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:56.000
These forms of misinformation prey on our mental blind spots and take advantage of our passions and loyalties. This week on Hidden Brain, the many insidious forms of misinformation, and how we can all get better at separating fact from fiction. If you have a computer or a smartphone, you have answers. Want to know what year Abraham Lincoln was born? Look it up. Curious who invented the windsock? You can find out in 30 seconds.

4
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:36.000
We have access to more information in a minute than our ancestors did in a lifetime. This privilege comes with a problem. How do we know if what we are seeing is true? All of us believe misinformation is something other people fall for. The uncle who gets his information from talk radio. Your high school classmate who trusts everything he sees on WhatsApp. The conspiracy theorist who imbibes lies on YouTube. At the London Business School, economist Alex Edmonds is interested in the science of misinformation. He says it's not just a fringe problem, misinformation is much more common than we think.

5
00:02:36.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Alex Edmonds, welcome to Hidden Brain. Thanks Shankar, it's really great to be here. Alex, you live in the UK. Some years ago, the British home stores made the news. BHS, as it's called, is a massive department store chain that sold everything from furniture to clothing. What happened in 2016 to get BHS in the news, Alex? So it effectively went bankrupt. And when they went bankrupt, they had unfunded pension deficits. So former employees who were owed pensions did not get them paid. So companies go bankrupt all the time, businesses fail all the time, but this became something

6
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:49.000
of a scandal, Alex. Can you explain why? What happened here that was not just a business going out of business, but something that felt like malfeasance was at play? Certainly you're absolutely right that just being bankrupt, that is just what business is. That's the nature of the beast. But here what happened was the former owner, Sir Philip Green, who was a rather larger than life character, he took out a lot of money and huge dividends to himself. And then he sold this company to a guy called Dominic Chappelle, who was a formerly bankrupt entrepreneur. He'd bankrupt further businesses in the past. And so by selling to him, he just put this in jeopardy and didn't care about all of the

7
00:03:49.000 --> 00:04:15.000
employees. So, Alex, Philip Green sells BHS to Dominic Chapelle. Thousands of people lose their jobs. Many more go without their full pensions. News outlets, meanwhile, report that Philip Green is awaiting delivery of a $150 million super yacht named Lionheart. Meanwhile, reports report that Philip Green is awaiting delivery of a $150 million superyacht named Lionheart. I'm imagining all this does not endear him to the British public. Yeah, that's correct. So again, the fact that a company goes bankrupt, that is part and parcel

8
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:53.000
of business. But if the company goes bankrupt and former employees don't have their pensions, and yet there's the person who's got a huge show of extravagance, this is a big problem. and yet there's the person who's got a huge show of extravagance, this is a big problem. So around the same time as the BHS candle, another UK chain, Sports Direct, faced its own set of controversies. Do you remember what happened, Alex? Yeah, so here we had a similar situation with a rather larger-than-life character. So the chairman is a guy called Mike Ashley, who also owned Newcastle United Football Club and therefore was very much in the public eye. But Sports Direct was under pressure for a number of reasons and one of them was not paying the minimum wage and that

9
00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:21.000
was just one of many bad workplace practices. So these scandals prompted a parliamentary hearing and you were summoned to testify in front of the select committee on business. Now, you've been long interested in corporate governance, executive pay, making a case for responsible business practices. Was this why you were called to testify, Alex? You're absolutely right, Shankar. This is in my field.

10
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:50.000
This is in my strike zone. I'm an academic, but I'm really passionate about using academic research for practical purposes so I submitted some written evidence to the inquiry. And based on the quality of the evidence submissions, the select committee then calls some witnesses to testify orally in order to delve deeper in the questions that they're trying to address. So as you were listening to another witness, you heard something that caught your attention. Tell me what it was, Alex.

11
00:05:50.000 --> 00:06:12.000
Yeah, so I got in early, perhaps because I was nervous. I needed to swat up on everything I'd written in my testimony just to make sure that if they asked me any questions that I was prepared. But I still listened with one ear open to what was happening. And there was a witness, the Trades Union's Congress, and what they mentioned was some research which sounded really noteworthy.

12
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:42.000
They said, the lower the pay gap between the CEO and the average employee, the better the company performance. And why did my ears prick up when I heard this? Because this was something I really wanted to be true. It shined with my views on responsible business. As I just discussed, in many cases, the more responsibly you act towards wider society and your employees, the better the company performance. So this seemed to be music to my ears.

13
00:06:42.000 --> 00:07:04.000
So you were happy to hear about this research that suggested that the narrower the pay gap between the CEO and the average worker, the better the company did. What prompted you then to do the next step of actually looking up the origins of this claim? I'd say it was entirely selfish. So I thought this was a great study. I would like to quote this study. So I wasn't coming out to try and check this or to catch anybody out, but I thought great

14
00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:39.000
study let me know what the study is. So the next time that I am to talk to companies about the importance of responsibility, I have another weapon in my arsenal. And so, well, how did I look this up? Well, the witness had, like me, submitted some written evidence beforehand. So I went to the written evidence and I found the paper that they cited. And then I looked up the paper on my iPad and then the paper seemed to say completely the opposite. Actually the greater the gap between CEO and Worker Pay, the better the company performance.

15
00:07:39.000 --> 00:08:17.000
I was confused. I thought maybe because I was so nervous because of my own session, did I misread everything? Perhaps because academic papers are not known for their clarity. But no, it was there. As clear as day, the greater the pay gap, the better the company performance. What do you think happened? Did they actually just misread the paper? So that would be one interpretation is that they just misquoted it but actually it wasn't as bad as that. What I noticed was I was looking at the 2013 version of the paper but what they quoted in their written

16
00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:54.000
evidence was the 2010 version of the paper and when I looked up the 2010 version of the paper it indeed had the opposite result, the one they originally quoted. So what had changed between 2010 and 2013 was the manuscript had gone through peer review, where the peer review process forces the authors to correct any mistakes. Once they corrected their mistakes they found completely the opposite result. Once they corrected their mistakes, they found completely the opposite result. Notice that the inquiry was in 2016. So the 2013 paper was already out there for three years. Well, of course, it could have just been an oversight.

17
00:08:54.000 --> 00:09:24.000
They happened to come by the 2010 paper. They didn't realize the 2013 paper was out there. You finished your testimony. It went well, but you decided that you were going to try and correct the record and help the committee basically get the correct paper. And so you went up to them afterwards and told them about the 2013 paper. How did you do this? What happened? Yes, so there's a clerk to the select committee who's not a member of Parliament but a senior civil servant, and he I was quite cordial with because he was the person I'd interacted with in terms of inviting me

18
00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:54.000
to testify. And I mentioned this and he seemed horrified. He's deemed this was really bad. Even if it was an honest mistake, he wanted to make sure that this inquiry conducted by the UK government had the best evidence. And he invited me to submit a supplement to my initial written evidence, responding to what I'd heard in the earlier session, I did so and the committee published it. So that's very promising. Is that how the committee eventually ended up its conclusions or does the story go further

19
00:09:54.000 --> 00:10:29.000
than this? Unfortunately not. There is a twist. So even though the committee published my supplementary evidence, which suggests they endorsed that what I'd written, the final report of the inquiry, which was made after considering all the written submissions and also the oral evidence in the hearings, still referred to the overturned result as if it was gospel, the idea that lower pay gaps lead to better company performance, and this led to them recommending that all companies disclose their pay gaps, and that

20
00:10:29.000 --> 00:11:01.000
recommendation eventually did become law. So this was something with some actual consequences. And in some ways, I think I can understand how this came about. This is coming on the heels of these major scandals where you have these leaders of these companies behaving in ways that are, you know, if not unethical then clearly perhaps improper. Certainly it didn't pass the the seamliness test, if you will. A lot of people lost their jobs, a lot of people had their pension benefits reduced, politicians got involved, Parliament got upset, and they wanted to hear the story that basically

21
00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:31.000
said, you know, you really need to rein in CEO behavior. And so in some ways, a study that basically said smaller pay gaps between the CEO and the median worker seem like it fit that larger thesis. Do you think that's how it came about, that that's what the driving impetus was for this error to persist in the final report? I do think so. So I don't think the committee set to deliberately misrepresent the evidence. I think that why did they start the inquiry? They did believe that CEOs were overpaid and they think that overpayment is a bad thing,

22
00:11:31.000 --> 00:12:26.000
that indeed was potentially a contributor to the two scandals of BHS and Sports Direct. Therefore, any evidence that they saw, they interpreted to be supportive of this. And if there was evidence against that, they might have dismissed that evidence because it just didn't sound right, it didn't feel right and so they responded to the evidence selectively. When we come back the psychological drivers of misinformation. You're listening to Hidden Brain, I'm Shankar Vedantam. This is Hidden Brain, I'm Shankar Vedantam. There are reams of psychological research on the power of stories.

23
00:12:26.000 --> 00:13:01.000
A compelling emotional narrative can capture our attention and persuade us in ways that data and reasoning often cannot. But what happens when our feelings are contradicted by the facts? Economist Alex Edmonds has spent years immersed in the world of data, science and math. He's found that there are psychological forces that make misinformation hard to fight. Alex, you were once cleaning up your junk email folder when you came across a message that stopped you in your tracks. What was this message?

24
00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:29.000
So this was a message from one of the leading investors in the world. So I'll call this investor Xinyi. So Xinyi had heard about my research showing that companies with high employee satisfaction beat their peers in terms of total shareholder return over a 28-year period. And she was interested in launching a fund based not in employee satisfaction but gender diversity and wondered if I could do a similar study showing app performance of more diverse companies.

25
00:13:29.000 --> 00:14:03.000
So in the earlier work that you had done, Alex, you had examined the hundred best companies in America and looked at sort of what might be driving their success. And you found this correlation between employee satisfaction and company success. Did you find that employee satisfaction was the driver of company performance or just that the two things were correlated? This is really important because as we know correlation does not imply causation and we need to be particularly careful when there's a danger of confirmation bias. We'd love to believe that there's good karma in the world, that companies that treat their

26
00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:40.000
workers better do better in the long term, but it could be the opposite. It could be that once a company starts doing well, then it can start investing in its employees. Or maybe there's causality in neither direction. There's a third factor that affects both. So maybe a great manager makes her employees happy because she's a great leader, and a great manager makes some good strategic decisions, and that leads to better company performance. So a lot of a job of an academic is to make sure that we really nail a result. In order to get published in a top scientific journal, we need to address those alternative explanations,

27
00:14:40.000 --> 00:15:10.000
and that's all the blood and sweat and tears that I had to shed in order to get this paper published. Alex agreed to meet the famous investor. She invited him to the Dorchester Hotel in Park Lane, London. If you play Monopoly, the most expensive square on the Monopoly board is Mayfair. The second most is Park Lane. So this is really real estate. And so Park Lane is a road which overlooks Hyde Park, a huge, beautiful park in London.

28
00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:48.000
And the Dorchester Hotel was a plush hotel. I think right now it would cost £800 per night to stay there. So the lobby had high ceilings, it had big windows, lots of light was flowing through. So it looked to be a beautiful hotel, the sort of hotel that one of the leading investors in the world would be staying at. So what did Shunyi want? So she mentioned that she was potentially going to be launching this new fund based on gender diversity. And she heard of my prior study. So she first asked whether I could directly tell her whether that study already showed that diversity led to outperformance.

29
00:15:48.000 --> 00:16:23.000
And I said no, because employee satisfaction covers many things, not just diversity, but things like fair pay, credibility, respect, fairness, pride and camaraderie. So she said, well, could I not adapt my methodology and rather than looking at employee satisfaction, look at diversity as being the driver? And I said, yeah, potentially. And indeed, there's a lot of measures out there of diversity that we can look at. It could be diversity in the boardroom, diversity in the work, the workforce. It could be winning a working mother or similar award.

30
00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:40.000
It could be the number of gender controversies in the news. And so you decided to take on this study. What did you think was exciting about the idea? What did you think that you would get out of it? It was the potential to partner with her in the launch of this fund. So clearly it was a preliminary discussion. We didn't discuss any terms or anything,

31
00:16:40.000 --> 00:17:17.000
but the reason for the meeting was implicitly that I could help her launch the fund. And this would have been amazing for my career. So not only might this be good financially, but I would be catapulted in to the mainstream from the academic backwaters in which I was used to working in, and also it's something I cared about intrinsically. So as an ethnic minority, I believe diversity be very important and if indeed you could launch a fund which does well for society in terms of promotion of females, if this led to higher financial returns, that would be a win-win. And that would be good for me intrinsically in addition to the instrumental benefits of

32
00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:53.000
financial returns and also perhaps greater recognition. So what did you do? Did you launch such a study, Alex? I did. I did do that study and there were 24 different measures of female friendliness available and so I worked with one of my MBA students at Wharton who was somebody who was a stellar student who got an A plus in my class. He had also told me that he was really interested in ethical business. So we worked together correlating these 24 different measures of performance with long-term financial returns. And what did you find?

33
00:17:53.000 --> 00:18:31.000
So unfortunately, we did not find what we hoped to find. So the vast majority of these measures found a negative relationship with long-term shirhold returns. There were only two measures which found a positive relationship. One of them was insignificant. So what that means is that the increase in returns was so small that it could have been due to luck rather than gender equality. But there was one result where there was a significant relationship between female friendliness and long-term shelter returns. So what did you do? What did you go back and tell the investor who

34
00:18:31.000 --> 00:19:04.000
had asked for the study? What did you report? Well, what we should have done was just report the two ones that were successful. And that would have been great because then I might have partnered with her in the launch of this fund, but that would not be correct. So my job is a researcher and as a researcher, you can't just bury some results because they don't support what you'd like to find. So we reported all of the results, including the huge number, which unfortunately found no relationship or a negative relationship. And we sent this to Shin Yi.

35
00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:48.000
Did Shin Yi accept the results? What was her reaction? She seemed disappointed but she seemed to accept that this is just what the results were. She thanked me and then that was the end of the project. Was this the end of the story though? It unfortunately was not. So a few months later I saw a press release where Xin Yu was launching a new fund based on gender diversity and this was the very thesis that she'd asked me to study. And this was surprising because in the press release announcement it said that there was a strong relationship between gender diversity and long-term financial performance, which was the very thesis that I had found not to be true.

36
00:19:48.000 --> 00:20:23.000
So my results overall did not support it, but what she did find was some study done by a commercial company which suggested there was a positive link between gender and financial performance. financial performance. You call this idea data mining and you say that both researchers as well as advocacy organizations are sometimes guilty of this. Can you explain what data mining is? Yeah, so data mining is that you start with a preferred result that you want to find and then you mine the data.

37
00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:44.000
You run the data in so many different ways until you get a positive result. So here, there were so many measures of performance that you could have tried, also so many ways that you could have measured diversity, and what she was able to find was a company which had delivered a positive result. Now here, the data mining was not done by Xingyi,

38
00:20:44.000 --> 00:21:21.000
it was done by the company, but why might the company have wanted to get a positive result? Because the idea that diverse companies perform better, that's something that people want to be true. If indeed you deliver the result, you might be picked up and indeed their paper was picked up indeed by Xinyi and that led to positive publicity for that organization. Now I just want to clarify Alex that you're not saying that you think CEOs should get exorbitant payouts in excess of their workers or that diversity is a bad thing. You're just saying we need to trust the evidence as it comes in and report it accurately.

39
00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:46.000
Absolutely and this is irrespective of your personal views on the topic. So personally, I believe that diversity is important. It's an important thing as an ethnic minority. It's something which is dear to me. But I believe that as a scientist, you should be like an expert witness in a criminal trial. Your role as a scientist is to state the evidence, just like your role as a witness

40
00:21:46.000 --> 00:22:34.000
is to state the evidence clearly irrespective of your views of the issue. I want to talk about another driver of misinformation. Tell me the story of a woman named Belle Gibson. Belle Gibson was a young Australian. She had cancer and this cancer was terminal. She had tried radiotherapy and chemotherapy and none of that worked. So instead she tried clean eating and she was blogging about her journey and she made a miraculous recovery. She was cured from her cancer. And this story went viral. It was shared it could have reached millions and it encouraged other people to shun traditional medicine, chemotherapy and radiotherapy, for diet and exercise.

41
00:22:34.000 --> 00:23:11.000
She got a book deal and this book deal was with Penguin Australia to have a cookbook showing what she did in order to cure herself from cancer. She also launched a healthy eating app called The Whole Pantry with recipes which were apparently curative of some of the most serious diseases. I mean, this is a fairy tale story, Alex. So someone has cancer, they cure themselves with cancer, they become a bestselling author, a successful entrepreneur. An amazing story, Alex. Is there any problem with it? Yes, there's one problem and sadly a big problem. She never had cancer.

42
00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:43.000
So this was a lie people shared a story without ever checking whether it was true. Some of the story is also about us, isn't it, Alex, which is that we wanted to believe that story, that story was appealing to us. Part of the reason she became a successful author and entrepreneur is because the story she was telling was a story that we actually wanted to hear. Absolutely. This was another example of confirmation bias. So why did we want to believe Belle's story?

43
00:23:43.000 --> 00:24:03.000
Because what it suggested is that you can achieve anything you want to, you can defeat even cancer if you just put your mind to it. And this is what we tell our kids, we tell them you can do anything you put your mind to. And so the idea that she was shunning traditional medicine, these were the chemicals that you get injected with if you're doing curing therapy,

44
00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:36.000
for something like clean eating. And that again, accords with our worldview that something natural is better than the medicines of a giant corporation. So it sort of made sense to us. And she was inspiring. And so she inspired everybody that even if you were at a death store, you could have a second chance of life. We love those underdog stories. They are in the fairy tales we learn as kids, we read as kids, but this was apparently not a fairy tale. This was somebody who we could see, looked really well and was eating healthily.

45
00:24:36.000 --> 00:25:24.000
So you say that the Bella Gibson story is an example of something called the narrative fallacy. What is the narrative fallacy here, Alex? The narrative fallacy is to see some events and believe that there is a link between the two, is that we can weave in a narrative which supports our view of the world. So if we see, claim that she had cancer, now claim that she didn't have cancer anymore, was cured, and we can see that she was clean eating, we weave in the explanation that we want, which is the clean eating cured her cancer. Now there's alternative explanations to this, and an alternative explanation could be that she lied because nobody had ever checked a claim of cancer, or even if she was cured,

46
00:25:24.000 --> 00:26:00.000
it could have been that she did other things to have been cured or maybe she didn't lie but she was misdiagnosed in the first place but those narratives don't feel so good to us so we latch on to the one narrative that we like just going back to the criminal trial example it would be like the police latching on to one particular suspect because of some particular prejudice and ignoring the alternative suspects. You know, we've talked elsewhere on the show about how the brain is a sense-making machine and we are in some ways evolutionarily wired to draw connections and see patterns and seek

47
00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:41.000
out explanations. So we may have all of the world's information at our fingertips, but in some ways we still have the same brain to make sense of it all. So Daniel Kahneman in his book Thinking Fast and Slow said there are two systems. There's the system two, which is rational. It knows to check the facts before ditching chemotherapy, check that this person actually did have cancer and was cured from clean eating. But there's the system one, which is the part of the brain that is activated by a fight or flight response. And that is one where we believe what we want to be true and we're not acting in a discerning way. So even really smart people who most of the time can activate

48
00:26:41.000 --> 00:27:36.000
their system too, and this is a really well-functioning system, when indeed our emotions take over, we forget basic things like correlation is not causation, check the facts, or are there other narratives than the one that we're trying to assign? So we talked earlier about how, you know, the evidence can sometimes be unreliable because researchers and policymakers can pick and choose their findings. But even when the data holds up, we sometimes can use data to make claims that are not necessarily supported by the data. You have a story about this. Tell me about the day that your son Casper was born and everything you had learned about raising a newborn child. Yeah, so this was an amazing day as anybody who's experienced the birth of a child will know and so we had huge positive

49
00:27:36.000 --> 00:28:23.000
emotions but then we realized we need to look after this little guy and one of the big questions is what to feed him and And my wife and I thought, well, this is a no-brainer. We had taken a national trial birth trust parenting course and the NCT is extremely well respected within the UK. And they talked about many things such as changing nappies, what to do if the baby is sick, but there were two classes devoted to breastfeeding. And while much of this was on how to breastfeed, it started with why breastfeed. The idea being that breastfeeding is tough, so if they convinced us of the benefits, then even if we were bleary-eyed and weary and wanted to reach for the rattle, we wouldn't because of the benefits. And so what they gave was a message which was also reinforced

50
00:28:23.000 --> 00:29:00.000
by the World Health Organization which said that you should exclusively breastfeed at least for the first six months, no water, no formula, no other fluids. And so why did they give this advice? Because of a strong correlation based on data. And what was that correlation? Babies who were breastfed exclusively had better outcomes later in life. This could be mental outcomes such as child IQ, that could be physical outcomes as well, such as reduction in allergies and other potential ailments. So in some ways the data linking breastfeeding with

51
00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:25.000
good outcomes in life is truly voluminous. I mean, they're probably warehouses of studies now that show the connection between these two things. But in the early days of your son's life, your doctors recommended feeding your child newborn formula along with breast milk. What was the reasoning here? Yeah, so the obstetrician said that in Casper's case,

52
00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:42.000
because he was born a little bit underweight, that it was important to top him up with formula. And also because he was born a little bit early, there was the risk of jaundice. So what is this? This is when there's lots of bilirubin in the system, and bilirubin is something which, if it accumulates, can cause jaundice.

53
00:29:42.000 --> 00:30:17.000
And in babies born early, this is particularly prevalent. And so the advantage of putting formula in is that that would flush out the bilirubin and reduce the risk of jaundice. So to us, it was not as black and white as the World Health Organization or the NCT course was suggesting. So what did you do? On the one hand, you have these warehouses of data telling you that breastfeeding is the only way to go. And your doctor is basically saying, you know, breastfeeding and formula. What did you do? So I did my own research and I thought this is my job as an academic. If I do this in my day job,

54
00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:41.000
shouldn't I also do this research in something as important as my child's well-being? So I did some Google searches myself. So I didn't just take at face value the studies quoted by the NCT, because as you're mentioning, if you have a particular viewpoint, you can find the study to support that viewpoint. But if I Google breastfeeding IQ, I found lots of studies which also reinforced the result

55
00:30:41.000 --> 00:31:25.000
that the NCT course had suggested. But then when I clicked on those particular studies, it actually gave a more nuanced picture. So what they found is that if you did a simple comparison of breastfed versus bottle-fed kids, then there was a difference in IQ and other outcomes. But what some studies also did was they realised that you needed to make adjustments. Why? Because breastfed and bottle-fed babies differ according to many other dimensions, not just the feeding method. So breastfeeding is really tough. It can be difficult without family support. So maybe it was babies with a better home life, a superior home environment, that were breastfed.

56
00:31:25.000 --> 00:32:07.000
And it could be that home environment led to the better outcomes, not the feeding methodology. Also, older mothers tended to breastfeed. It could be the mother's more educated. There were other things such as the mother who was less likely to smoke is also more likely to breastfeed. And it could be all of those factors which were behind the child outcomes not the feeding method. And in fact when these studies controlled for those other factors what that means is when they stripped out the influence of those other factors on IQ and the other outcomes the link between breastfeeding and IQ was pretty much zero. So again I don't think what I'm hearing you saying is that breastfeeding is ineffective

57
00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:35.000
or useless. You're basically saying that black and white thinking about breastfeeding might be the problem here. That is correct. And so why is this such an important issue is that many women feel guilt tripped into only breastfeeding and they feel guilty if they are considering something else. And this is problematic because it means that if they're exhausted then they are still trying to breastfeed and that exhaustion can actually be bad not just for the mother but also for the child's

58
00:32:35.000 --> 00:33:11.000
development. It also means that as a father you can't get as involved in feeding and feeding is one way to bond with your child and so the message which is often perpetrated, which is never do anything other than breastfeeding is where we're a careful look at the data is really important. The world makes it easy for us to find facts that confirm our pre-existing beliefs and seek out stories that tell us our values and our hearts are in the right place.

59
00:33:11.000 --> 00:33:55.000
So how can we know whether to trust what we read, watch and hear? When we come back, strategies for sifting through the information coming at us every day and fighting back against falsehoods and bad actors. You're listening to Hidden Brain. I'm Shankar Vedanta. This is Hidden Brain. I'm Shankar Vedantam. When we think of misinformation, we think of politicians making outlandish claims, or self-proclaimed experts selling snake oil.

60
00:33:55.000 --> 00:34:34.000
But in reality, misinformation isn't always obvious and not easily avoidable. Much of the time the things we fall for are shaped by our own psychological needs and biases. Economist Alex Edmonds is the author of the book May Contain Lies, How Stories, Statistics and Studies Exploit Our Biases and What We Can Do About It. Alex has a powerful example of misinformation in his own life. He once watched an inspiring talk given by an acrobat with the entertainment company Cirque du Soleil.

61
